course: dictionary
lesson: 3657
title: Fiscal: words about government money
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Fiscal is a formal word. It means about government money: the taxes a government collects and the money it spends on things like schools, roads and hospitals. When you see «fiscal» in the news, ask yourself: is this about the government's money?
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Fiscal consolidation means cuts in public spending and tax rises.» The clues are cuts, spending and tax rises, all about money that a government takes in or pays out. So fiscal is linked to spending and taxes.
explain: Some words go with fiscal. A fiscal year is a twelve-month period that a government or company uses to count its money. In the US people say fiscal year; in the UK and Australia they say financial year, which means the same thing.
fact: Fiscal means about government money: taxes and public spending.
fact: «Fiscal policy» means the choices a government makes about its taxes and spending.
fact: A «fiscal year» is a twelve-month period used for keeping accounts.
fact: «Fiscal consolidation» means cuts in public spending and tax rises.
fact: Everyday words for the same idea are «money» or «financial»; fiscal is a formal word you will mostly meet in the news and in economics.
exercise: «Fiscal consolidation means cuts in public spending and tax rises.» What does fiscal consolidation mean? | Building new parks and playgrounds for free | Saving money in a piggy bank for a holiday | *Cuts in public spending and tax rises | Giving everyone a pay rise
exercise: A news report says «The government's fiscal year begins in April.» What is a fiscal year? | *A twelve-month period used to count a government's money | A holiday from school in the spring | A famous festival | A season when it snows a lot
exercise: Nadia says fiscal is just a posh word for money. Why might a news writer choose fiscal? | *It means money that belongs to the government, which is more exact | It is a shorter word | It means exactly the same as sad | It means happy
test: «Fiscal policy» is about which of these? | Playing football in the park | *Taxes and government spending | Choosing a new pet | Cooking dinner in the kitchen
test: In the UK and Australia, a twelve-month period for accounts is usually called a... | birthday year | fiscal holiday | *financial year | sports season
exam: A news story says «cuts in public spending and tax rises» are called fiscal consolidation. What does fiscal mean here? | Something about the sea and the weather | Something about cooking and food | Something about playing sport | *Something about government money
